WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.075
Why act at all?

2
00:00:01.075 --> 00:00:04.113
Start with the two decisions
programs keep merging.

3
00:00:04.113 --> 00:00:06.512
Choosing a pipe is a physics decision.

4
00:00:06.512 --> 00:00:11.101
Light in fiber covers about one
kilometer every five microseconds.

5
00:00:11.101 --> 00:00:14.075
New York to London has
a round-trip floor of 56

6
00:00:14.075 --> 00:00:17.613
milliseconds before any
equipment touches a packet.

7
00:00:17.613 --> 00:00:22.566
The well-known 150-millisecond voice
budget is mouth to ear, and the phones

8
00:00:22.566 --> 00:00:27.438
themselves take a large share, which
leaves the network roughly 70 to 100.

9
00:00:27.438 --> 00:00:31.469
On the book's worked arithmetic, a
Seattle office hairpinning through

10
00:00:31.469 --> 00:00:35.500
northern Virginia spends about 60
milliseconds one way on geography.

11
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.050
That's most of the allowance, gone
before the call reaches anything useful.

12
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.887
No product moves that floor.

13
00:00:41.887 --> 00:00:45.788
Local breakout fixes the
geographic part and nothing else.

14
00:00:45.788 --> 00:00:49.038
Choosing what governs the pipe
is a compliance decision.

15
00:00:49.038 --> 00:00:51.773
Direct internet access solves the hairpin,

16
00:00:51.773 --> 00:00:54.888
and it satisfies none of
the protection controls.

17
00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:59.278
Boundary protection, flow enforcement,
transmission confidentiality,

18
00:00:59.278 --> 00:01:03.376
malware inspection and monitoring
all come from the control layer.

19
00:01:03.376 --> 00:01:09.338
Transport selection closes exactly one
control family: telecommunications diversity.

20
00:01:09.338 --> 00:01:12.200
So here's the risk an
executive should watch for.

21
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.875
A branch moved from a governed
hairpin to bare local breakout has

22
00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:19.550
lost assessed boundary protection
relative to where it started.

23
00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:23.600
The book's phrase for that is a compliance
regression with better latency.

24
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.358
It looks like modernization
on a dashboard,

25
00:01:26.358 --> 00:01:28.962
and it reads as a
finding in an assessment.

26
00:01:28.962 --> 00:01:32.683
Every decision in this briefing
keeps those two choices separate:

27
00:01:32.683 --> 00:01:34.509
buy the pipe on measured physics,

28
00:01:34.509 --> 00:01:38.838
and take the protection from the governor
and the security services above it.

29
00:01:38.838 --> 00:01:43.097
Each new pipe also adds a managed
interface someone has to govern,

30
00:01:43.097 --> 00:01:47.570
so more circuits without more control
means more exposure, not less.
